{# How a generated page links THE SCRIPT — one tag, to whichever bundle the
   CALLER asked for. It lives beside the bundle it loads;
   css/css.loader.html.j2 is its twin, and both shells include the pair:

     {% include "css/css.loader.html.j2" %}
     {% include "js/js.loader.html.j2" %}

   `asset_bundles` and `local_href` mean exactly what they mean in the twin;
   the stylesheet half documents them once.

   ONE SCRIPT, NO FALLBACK. Until 12.0.0 this half carried the harder version of
   the local-first trick, and it is worth recording why it was hard: a <script>
   that has already failed will NOT re-fetch when its `src` is reassigned, so
   the handler had to append a FRESH element instead. Getting that wrong looked
   like it worked — the assignment is silent — and the page simply had no
   JavaScript. None of it is needed once the page is TOLD which href to write,
   and the asymmetry with the stylesheet went with it.

   `defer` stays. bundle.js injects its modules and must not block the parse,
   and js/modules/main.js still checks `document.readyState` before it
   initialises — which costs nothing and keeps it correct whenever it lands. #}
{% if asset_bundles == "local" %}
  <script src="{{ local_href }}/js/bundle.js" defer></script>
{% else %}
  <script src="{{ cdn_href }}/js/bundle.js" defer></script>
{% endif %}
